  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 303 AM MDT Wed Oct 28 2026\n\nThe forecast is again working out fairly well this morning as \nsome jet enhanced precipitation is currently working its way \nacross roughly the southern half of the area, including here at \nthe humble Riverton Weather Abode. It is rain at our office \nright now, but there has been some snow in places like Pinedale \nand Big Piney. A few lightning strikes have also been noted. \nAlmost all showers should end by sunrise today. However, there \nwill likely be some slick spots on roads that saw snow. And, a \nfew locations have seen the fog monster crawl out of this lair \nto feed on the moisture from the precipitation.\n\nOnce the showers end, ridging should build across the area and \nbring at least two days of fairly tranquil weather with many \nareas having three days of fairly quiet weather, lasting through\nFriday. There will still be somewhat of a pressure gradient in \nthe wind prone areas from Muddy Gap through Casper so a gusty \nwind will continue there, but not nearly as strong as yesterday.\nWind should decrease through Friday as well. Temperatures will \nalso be on the rise. The temperatures in the forecast today are \nprobably the coolest it will be. I could see it 3 to 5 degrees \nhigher on each day. The warmest day looks to be Friday when \nplaces like Casper may be well up into the 60s. \n\nThe next chance of showers will begin to move into western \nWyoming as a trough rotates around an upper level low off of the\nPacific Northwest Coast and brings increasing moisture to the \narea. There continues to be disagreement in the guidance in \nregards to placement and intensity of the showers, with some \nmodels, like the GFS, keeping things mainly dry through most of \nSaturday. For now, most areas have some chance on Saturday but \nmost places should be dry most of the time.\n\nThe most interesting feature of the forecast though is from \nSunday into Monday. There is an interesting and complex \nsituation setting up. We have two main players. The first is the\naforementioned upper level low that will get kicked eastward \ninto the Rockies Sunday night and into Monday. Another player is\nan area of low pressure from further south in the Pacific Ocean\nthat will move into southern California on Sunday and track \neast to northeastward into the Southern Rockies. The big \nquestion is how will these systems interact, how much moisture \nfrom the much wetter southern system can get drawn northward and\nif the systems phase and where will they phase. The guidance of\nboth the deterministic and ensemble varieties, continues to  \nhave a rather wide spread in regards to all of these variables. \nIn the deterministic realm, the ECMWF phases the systems \nfurther eastward and is slower, which would mean a wetter \nsolution. The GFS is faster and drier, with the phasing and \ndeepening of the low further east. So, although we are fairly \nconfident of at least some unsettled weather across the area, \nprecipitation amounts and the placement and timing of \nprecipitation remains highly uncertain. A first look at the NBM \nensemble gives at least a 1 in 2 chance for most areas to get at\nleast a quarter of an inch or QPF from Sunday morning through \nMonday evening. As for snow, the 1 in 2 chance of 6 inches over\nthe same time frame is largely confined to the mountains above \n8000 feet. It could get cold enough for lower elevations to see \nthe first snowflakes of the year, but chances of decent \naccumulations of greater than 2 inches remains low, under 1 out \nof 4 at this time. But remember, this is still 5 days out so a \nlot can and probably will change. Stay tuned as we get closer \nto the event for more clarity. After this system departs, there \nis good model agreement in ridging building over the area, \npossibly bringing dry and tranquil conditions for much of next \nweek.",
